      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© Georg Thieme Verlag KG Stuttgart · New York.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "Few studies have investigated the likelihood of weight maintenance in obese persons with schizophrenia after their initial successful weight loss. This pilot open-label study examined the efficacy of topiramate in weight loss and the trajectory of weight changes after topiramate discontinuation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "This study enrolled 10 obese persons with schizophrenia. A 4-month treatment phase was started, followed by a 12-month discontinuation phase. Body weight was measured as the primary outcome every month. Secondary outcomes included leptin levels, fasting glucose, lipid profiles, and insulin resistance index."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "After the 4-month addition of topiramate, participants lost 1.79\u2009kg of their body weight (95% CI=-3.03 to -0.56, p=0.005). The maximum weight reduction was 4.32\u2009kg, occurring when topiramate had been discontinued for 12 months (95% CI=-6.41 to -2.24, p<0.001)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "DISCUSSION",
            "content": "The continuing weight-loss effect after topiramate discontinuation might have resulted from topiramate's potential to improve leptin functioning. These findings demonstrate that topiramate's weight-loss effect could not only persist during its administration, but also continue to improve after its discontinuation."
          }
        ]
      },
